  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nCloudy skies from a passing low will be found across the north \nfor much of tonight, while mainly clear skies are found south. \nSome breezy northwest winds may also continue into this evening \nfrom a passing cold front. Tonight, lows will be cooler and \ngenerally in the 20s. Generally dryer air is forecast to move \nin, although diminished winds and some lingering low level \nmoisture could return fog tonight into Thursday morning. Weak \nridging aloft will bring mainly dry conditions conditions for \nThursday. There are some indications a weak vort max could ride \nover this ridge later in the day, perhaps bringing some isolated\nrain showers. For now the forecast remains dry. Temperatures \nwill be slightly cooler yet near normal for Thursday behind \ntodays cold front. Ridging builds further for Friday, bringing \nanother day of dry conditions and mild temperatures. Some breezy\nsoutherly winds may also return in the west later Friday as the\nridge moves eastward.\n\nFriday night through Halloween will overall see mild conditions\nwith limited chances for precipitation. As the ridge pulls away\nsoutherly flow will bring increased low level moisture to the \narea with broad southwest trough like flow aloft. The result \ncould bring isolated rain showers, however NBM PoPs continue to \ntrend downward. The increased southerly flow and a weak warm \nfront could still bring mostly cloudy skies for Saturday, with \nperhaps some mist, patchy fog, and drizzle at times. \nTemperatures Saturday are forecast in the 50s for most areas, \nalthough may be dependent on cloud cover. Similar mild \nconditions with lower clouds could be found on Sunday, with some\nincreased southerly flow. Precipitation chances again look low,\nalthough a repeat of patchy fog, mist, and drizzle may be \npossible. Highs could enter into the 60s for Sunday, with \nmorning lows in the 40s. Clusters still indicate a broad cutoff \nlow will move across the central plains early next week. A split\nsurface low could provide for increased chances for mainly rain\nto start the week, then most clusters have a large low in the \nplains taking over. This would limit precipitation chances, \nwhile bringing some cooler conditions by mid next week. Some \nbreezy winds may also be found during the progression of this \ndisturbance, mainly to start the week.",
